export default "When your teen starts their period, a tracking app seems like an obvious tool. It helps them predict their cycle, understand their body, and feel more prepared. But most period trackers are built for adult users, with adult data collection practices and no safeguards for minors.\n\nThis assessment helps you evaluate readiness, choose wisely, and avoid handing a data-harvesting app to your teenager.\n\n## Question 1: Has Your Teen Started Menstruating?\n\n**Where are they in the process?**\n\n- **Option A: Not yet, but it's expected soon.** A tracker is premature. Focus on education and conversation. The app comes after there's something to track.\n- **Option B: Recently started (first few cycles).** A good time to introduce a simple tracker. Early cycles are irregular, so prediction accuracy will be low. Set that expectation.\n- **Option C: Has been menstruating for six months or more.** Enough history to start seeing patterns. A tracker adds structure to what they're already experiencing.\n\nEarly cycles are often irregular. A tracker that says \"your period is late\" when cycles are still establishing can cause needless anxiety. Choose an app that handles irregular cycles without alarming language.\n\n## Question 2: Does Your Teen Want a Tracker?\n\n**Whose idea is this?**\n\n- **Option A: They asked for one.** Best scenario. They're interested, motivated, and more likely to use it consistently.\n- **Option B: I'm suggesting it.** Fine, but present it as a tool, not an assignment. If they're not interested, don't push it.\n- **Option C: Their friends use one.** Peer influence is powerful. Make sure the friend's recommendation isn't driving them toward a specific app with poor privacy practices.\n\nA tracker imposed by a parent becomes a chore. A tracker chosen by the teen becomes a tool. Let them participate in the selection.\n\n## Question 3: What Features Does a Teen Actually Need?\n\n**Match features to their developmental stage.**\n\n- **Option A: Period prediction and a simple calendar.** This is enough for most teens. They want to know when their period is coming so they can be prepared.\n- **Option B: Period prediction plus basic symptom tracking (cramps, mood).** Adds body awareness. Useful if they're curious about patterns or if symptoms are affecting school or activities.\n- **Option C: Full feature set including fertility tracking.** Not appropriate for most teens who are just starting to menstruate. Fertility features add complexity and data sensitivity without serving their current needs.\n\nStart with the minimum. They can add features as their understanding and needs grow. Loading a beginner with BBT charts and cervical mucus fields is counterproductive.\n\n## Question 4: How Important Is Data Privacy for a Minor?\n\n**This is not a preference question. It's a protection question.**\n\n- **Option A: Very important. I want the least data collection possible.** Choose an on-device app with no account requirement. A minor's health data on a company's server can be breached, sold, or subpoenaed.\n- **Option B: Somewhat important. I'll read the privacy policy.** Good start. Check specifically for COPPA compliance, third-party SDK disclosures, and what data the app transmits.\n- **Option C: I hadn't considered it.** Consider it now. Teens are especially vulnerable to data collection because they're building digital footprints they can't easily undo. A reproductive health profile created at 13 follows them.\n\nOn-device storage reduces the category of readable server-side data risks for minors. It's the simplest and most protective choice.\n\n## Question 5: Will You Discuss Tracking with Your Teen?\n\n**The app is a conversation starter, not a replacement for one.**\n\n- **Option A: Yes, we'll set it up together and talk about what it does.** Ideal. Setting it up together teaches digital health literacy. Discuss what data means, what the app does with it, and that their cycle information is private.\n- **Option B: I'll hand them the app and let them figure it out.** They'll learn the interface but miss the context. They won't understand what data they're sharing or why it matters.\n- **Option C: I want to monitor their data through a shared account.** Shared access undermines the teen's health autonomy and their trust in tracking. If you need to discuss cycle concerns, have a conversation. Don't surveil.\n\nA period tracker is one of the first health tools a teen manages independently. How you introduce it sets a precedent for how they'll handle health data going forward.\n\n## Question 6: What's Your Budget?\n\n**What are you willing to spend on a teen's period tracker?**\n\n- **Option A: Free only.** Free apps monetize through ads or data. For a minor, this tradeoff is worse than it is for adults. Consider whether \"free\" is actually free.\n- **Option B: A reasonable one-time or subscription cost.** Paid apps with clean privacy practices exist. The cost buys your teen out of the ad-supported data economy.\n- **Option C: Whatever it takes for the most private option.** On-device apps often have simple pricing models. Privacy for a minor is worth paying for.\n\nThe financial cost of a good tracker is small compared to the cost of a minor's health data in the wrong hands.\n\n## Your Results\n\n**Your readiness assessment:**\n\n**Ready with the right app (Option B or C on Q1, A on Q2, A or B on Q3, A on Q4):** Your teen is menstruating, interested, and you're prioritizing privacy. Choose an on-device app with simple features. See [Floriva for teens](/app-guides/floriva-for-teens) and the [beginner guide quiz](/tools/quiz/period-tracking-beginner-guide-quiz) for setup guidance.\n\n**Ready with conversation first (B on Q2, B on Q5):** The tool readiness is there, but the context isn't. Have the conversation about what period tracking is, what data means, and why privacy matters before downloading anything. Then set it up together.\n\n**Not quite ready (A on Q1, C on Q2 without personal interest):** The timing isn't right. Focus on menstrual education and body literacy. Introduce the tracking tool when they have something to track and want to track it.\n\n**Reconsider your approach (C on Q3 or C on Q5):** If you're looking for fertility features for a young teen or planning to monitor their data, step back. A period tracker for a teen should be simple, private, and theirs to manage. Your role is to educate and support, not to surveil.\n\nThe best period tracker for a teen is one that teaches them to manage their own health data before the world teaches them not to care about it.";
